"""Extra depth for Fabrics hub articles (Oct 2026). Inserted before the closing call-to-action / sources. Add only."""
MORE = {
'fabrics-ss27-runway-fabric-report': [
 ('h2', 'From runway to range: a practical plan'),
 ('p', 'Most brands will not buy latex or ultrafine ripstop. The job is to translate the runway mood into fabrics that suit their customer, price and climate. A simple approach is to split the range into three tiers: a few statement pieces that show the trend clearly, a larger group of commercial pieces that carry a softer version, and core products updated with one detail.'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Statement:</b> organza overlays, sheer skirts and glossy technical outerwear.', '<b>Commercial:</b> voile and fine cotton shirting, lined sheer sleeves, crinkle and taffeta in small doses.', '<b>Core:</b> lighter weights in existing qualities, a sheen finish on a best-selling trouser, or a sheer trim on a basic top.']),
 ('h2', 'How the story changes by region'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Europe and North America:</b> sheer layering over opaque bases for spring, and technical gloss for transitional outerwear.', '<b>Middle East (GCC):</b> sheer used as layers over covered silhouettes: organza abayas, sleeves and overlays.', '<b>South and Southeast Asia:</b> breathable voile, mulmul and fine cotton for heat; organza for festive and wedding layers.', '<b>Southern hemisphere (Australia, South America, South Africa):</b> the SS27 fabric story lands in their spring/summer from September 2027, giving extra time to test it.']),
 ('h2', 'Quality checks for sheer and glossy fabrics'),
 ('ul', ['Check seam slippage on sheer fabrics; use French or bound seams.', 'Test coated and glossy finishes for cracking, peeling and colour transfer after washing.', 'Ask for opacity samples on skin tones, and plan linings where needed.', 'Confirm care labels: many glossy and coated fabrics cannot be tumble dried or hot pressed.']),
],
'fabrics-paris-ss27-featherlight': [
 ('h2', 'What "featherlight" means in a fabric brief'),
 ('p', 'Lightness is measurable. When you brief a mill, specify weight in grams per square metre (GSM), yarn count and construction rather than "light". Most featherlight shirting and dress fabrics sit below about 100 GSM; sheer organza and voile can be well under 60 GSM. Ultra-light technical nylons for outerwear are often in a similar range. Ask for the same quality in two or three weights to compare drape.'),
 ('h2', 'Construction tips for light fabrics'),
 ('ul', ['Use fine needles and threads to avoid puckering.', 'Choose French seams, narrow rolled hems or bound edges for a clean finish.', 'Interface lightly or use self-fabric facings so the outside stays soft.', 'Plan pattern pieces to reduce visible seams on sheer areas.']),
 ('h2', 'Colour on light fabrics'),
 ('p', 'Pastels and neutrals read softer on sheer bases because the skin or lining shows through. Approve lab dips over the colour of the intended lining or on skin-tone cards, not only on white. Tangerine and other brights shown in Paris need extra care: on voile they can look washed out, while on satin they can look much stronger.'),
 ('h2', 'Who should use it, and when'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Hot climates:</b> lightness is a year-round selling point; lead with breathable naturals.', '<b>Temperate markets:</b> sell lightness as a spring and summer layering story.', '<b>Occasion wear:</b> organza and crepe give volume and movement without weight for long events.']),
 ('h2', 'Forecut timing'),
 ('p', 'Weightless fabrics are rising for SS27. Forecut expects sheer layering to peak in SS27 and to ease in AW27, while lighter weights in natural fibres continue as a long-term direction linked to climate and comfort.'),
],
'fabrics-milan-ss27-sheer-gloss': [
 ('h2', 'Translating gloss for commercial ranges'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Coated cotton and nylon:</b> trench coats, bombers and shorts with a light sheen.', '<b>Crinkle nylon:</b> easy, packable trousers and skirts.', '<b>Taffeta:</b> eveningwear, bows and statement skirts.', '<b>Satin and sateen:</b> slip skirts and shirts that catch the light without plastic finishes.', '<b>Lacquered leather effects:</b> small doses in bags, belts and trims.']),
 ('h2', 'Sheer and gloss together'),
 ('p', 'The strongest Milan looks contrasted matte sheer layers with shiny solids. A commercial version might pair an organza blouse with a satin skirt, or a mesh layer over a glossy slip. The contrast of surface creates interest even in a single colour.'),
 ('h2', 'Sustainability questions to ask'),
 ('p', 'Many glossy finishes rely on coatings that can make fabrics harder to recycle. Ask suppliers what the coating is made of, whether it is free of PFAS and other restricted chemicals, and whether recycled or bio-based bases are available. Mono-material options, such as recycled nylon with a nylon-based coating, are easier to recycle.'),
 ('h2', 'Checks and stripes in shirting'),
 ('p', 'Milan\'s return of checks, stripes and tartan in vintage tones gives shirting buyers an easy update for SS27 and AW27. Yarn-dyed cottons in soft, buttery colours suit both menswear and womenswear, and work in hot climates as well as cooler ones.'),
 ('h2', 'Where it sells'),
 ('p', 'Glossy outerwear sells best in cooler, rainy climates: northern Europe, Japan, Korea and the US Northeast. In hot markets, use gloss in eveningwear and accessories rather than heavy coated garments.'),
],
'fabrics-lakme-delhi-2026-textiles': [
 ('h2', 'What provenance fibres need to succeed'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Clear origin:</b> state where the fibre was grown, spun and woven.', '<b>Maker credit:</b> name the cooperative or enterprise, with permission.', '<b>Fair pricing:</b> hand-spun and hand-woven fibres take time; price must cover it.', '<b>Care guidance:</b> pashmina and fine wools need gentle care; explain it on labels and product pages.', '<b>Authenticity:</b> use recognised marks where they exist, such as GI tags for certain textiles, and avoid vague claims.']),
 ('h2', 'Pashmina explained'),
 ('p', 'Pashmina is the fine undercoat fibre from Changthangi goats raised at high altitude in Ladakh. It is very soft, light and warm. Because supply is limited and processing is mostly by hand, authentic pashmina is expensive and often blended or imitated. Brands should be precise about fibre content and avoid using "pashmina" for blends unless the label states the mix.'),
 ('h2', 'Product ideas for AW27'),
 ('ul', ['Shawls and stoles for weddings and winter functions.', 'Lightweight jackets and waistcoats in Himalayan wool.', 'Wedding layers: pashmina dupattas and capes over silk lehengas.', 'Menswear: shawls and mufflers paired with bandhgalas.']),
 ('h2', 'The global angle'),
 ('p', 'Provenance and traceable natural fibres are a global luxury story, from Scottish cashmere to Andean alpaca. Indian Himalayan textiles can sit in the same category for international buyers, especially when the story, quality and traceability are documented.'),
 ('h2', 'Update note'),
 ('p', 'This story was written while the week was running. Forecut adds confirmed runway highlights to the fabric forecast as published coverage becomes available.'),
],
'fabrics-mill-report-aw27-28': [
 ('h2', 'How to use fair signals'),
 ('p', 'Fabric fairs show what mills are investing in, roughly 12 to 18 months ahead of stores. A signal seen at more than one fair, such as soft-focus surfaces or natural stretch, is more reliable than one seen at a single booth. Forecut weighs fair signals together with runway and retail data before moving a fabric trend to rising.'),
 ('h2', 'Questions to ask mills now'),
 ('ul', ['Which AW27-28 qualities are available in sample yardage, and from when?', 'What are the minimum order quantities for development colours?', 'Which qualities carry certifications (for example GOTS, GRS, RWS or FSC-sourced cellulose)?', 'What lead time should we plan for bulk?', 'Can the same quality be made in a lighter weight for warmer markets?']),
 ('h2', 'Regional reading'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Northern hemisphere:</b> AW27-28 fabrics land in stores from August 2027.', '<b>Southern hemisphere:</b> the same story suits autumn/winter from March 2028.', '<b>Hot climates:</b> take the surface ideas (blur, irregularity, soft colour) into lighter cottons and blends rather than heavy wools.']),
 ('h2', 'Budgeting for innovation'),
 ('p', 'New finishes and fibres often cost more at first. Many brands start with one hero quality in a small capsule, measure sell-through and customer feedback, then scale the successful options in the following season.'),
],
'fabrics-forecast-ss27-aw27': [
 ('h2', 'How to use this forecast'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Emerging:</b> sample now and test in small quantities; these will grow over the next seasons.', '<b>Rising:</b> commit to buys; these are gaining adoption across brands and markets.', '<b>Peaking:</b> keep in core ranges but plan the exit; demand is at its highest.', '<b>Fading:</b> reduce depth and avoid new development.']),
 ('p', 'Stages are global. Check your region page for local timing: southern-hemisphere markets sell the same season about six months later, and hot-climate markets often adopt lighter versions.'),
],
'fabrics-news-autumn-2026': [
 ('h2', 'Why PFAS-free matters now'),
 ('p', 'PFAS are a large family of fluorinated chemicals once common in water- and stain-repellent finishes. Several governments and many brands are restricting them because they persist in the environment. For suppliers, PFAS-free performance is moving from a selling point to a buyer requirement, especially for outerwear, outdoor and uniform fabrics.'),
 ('h2', 'What the new fibres mean in practice'),
 ('p', 'Biopolyesters and cellulose threads are still early. They matter because they could make everyday products, sportswear and basic shirts, easier to recycle or less polluting at end of life. Brands can prepare by asking suppliers about trials and by designing products with fewer mixed materials.'),
 ('h2', 'Living Matter in product'),
 ('ul', ['Irregular yarns and slubs in shirting and knitwear.', 'Uneven, hand-look dyeing in neutrals and cool tones.', 'Denim used in tailored jackets and trousers.', 'Upholstery-style jacquards in jackets and bags.']),
 ('h2', 'Handloom in the global market'),
 ('p', 'India\'s handloom sector is one of the largest sources of hand-woven cloth in the world. With traceability and quality documentation, handloom fits the global demand for natural, low-impact and craft-led fabrics, from summer shirting to home textiles.'),
 ('h2', 'Action list for the season'),
 ('ul', ['Audit water-repellent finishes in your outerwear.', 'Ask your top three suppliers about bio-based and recycled options.', 'Add one irregular-surface fabric to your AW27 development.', 'Document origin for any handloom or natural-fibre products.']),
],
'fabrics-upcoming-2027-28': [
 ('h2', 'Direction by direction'),
 ('ul', ['<b>PFAS-free performance:</b> expect it first in rainwear, travel and outdoor ranges; check wash durability before making claims.',
         '<b>Bio-based polyesters:</b> early volumes in activewear; useful where buyers ask for lower fossil content.',
         '<b>Mono-material construction:</b> matching threads, labels and trims in the same fibre family so garments can be recycled.',
         '<b>Regenerative cotton denim:</b> cotton from farms using soil-health practices, sold with traceability data.',
         '<b>Trompe-l\'oeil prints:</b> prints that look like texture (tweed, knit, lace) on light fabrics, ideal for warm climates.',
         '<b>Upholstery velvets and jacquards:</b> rich surfaces in jackets, eveningwear and bags.',
         '<b>Irregular, handmade-looking surfaces:</b> slubs, crinkles and uneven dyes that suggest craft.',
         '<b>Handloom as quiet luxury:</b> premium positioning with provenance, especially for summer shirting and occasion wear.']),
 ('h2', 'Timing'),
 ('p', 'Fair signals appear about a year before stores. The directions above were visible at the summer and autumn 2026 fairs and are expected in collections from SS27 (in early form) through AW28. Forecut marks the stage of each on the fabric forecast.'),
 ('h2', 'Matching directions to customers'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Value brands:</b> trompe-l\'oeil prints and irregular surfaces offer a fresh look at low cost.', '<b>Premium brands:</b> handloom, regenerative cotton and velvets carry a story worth a higher price.', '<b>Performance and outdoor:</b> PFAS-free and bio-based synthetics are becoming essential.', '<b>Exporters:</b> mono-material and traceability prepare you for buyer and regulatory requirements in Europe.']),
 ('h2', 'A testing plan'),
 ('p', 'Choose one direction per category. Sample two or three qualities, wear-test them, check them in your own washing and colour tests, and launch a small capsule. Use sell-through and returns data to decide what to scale.'),
],
'fabrics-prints-guide': [
 ('h2', 'Choosing a method: cost, minimums and lead time'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Hand block:</b> low set-up cost per block, modest minimums, longer lead times, visible handmade character.', '<b>Screen (flatbed):</b> screens needed for each colour; good for bold graphics and medium runs.', '<b>Rotary:</b> higher set-up, lowest unit cost at scale; best for large repeat orders.', '<b>Digital:</b> almost no set-up, unlimited colours, ideal for sampling, short runs and photographic artwork; unit cost is higher at volume.']),
 ('h2', 'Repeats and placement'),
 ('p', 'All-over prints run in a repeat, the size of the pattern before it starts again. Screen and rotary repeats are limited by screen size; digital prints can have very large repeats. Placement or engineered prints are designed for a particular pattern piece, such as a border on a saree, a scarf print or a print that follows the hem of a dress.'),
 ('h2', 'Matching print and fabric'),
 ('ul', ['Pigment prints sit on the surface and suit cottons and blends; they can feel slightly stiffer.', 'Reactive dyes bond with cellulose fibres (cotton, linen, viscose) and give soft, bright results.', 'Acid dyes suit silk and wool.', 'Disperse dyes and sublimation suit polyester.']),
 ('h2', 'Print trends to watch'),
 ('p', 'Forecut tracks trompe-l\'oeil textures, faded and vintage florals, and hand-block motifs used on modern silhouettes. For warm climates, prints that imitate heavier textures let customers wear the look in light fabrics.'),
 ('h2', 'Quality checks'),
 ('ul', ['Rub fastness (dry and wet).', 'Wash fastness and colour bleeding, especially for strong reds and indigos.', 'Registration: colours lining up correctly in multi-colour prints.', 'Hand feel after printing and washing.']),
],
'fabrics-embroidery-bases': [
 ('h2', 'Stabilisers and backings'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Tear-away:</b> removed after stitching; for stable wovens.', '<b>Cut-away:</b> stays in the garment; for knits and stretchy fabrics that need ongoing support.', '<b>Wash-away or water-soluble:</b> dissolves after stitching; for sheer fabrics, lace effects and free-standing work.', '<b>Fusible interfacing:</b> adds body to light fabrics before hand or machine embroidery.']),
 ('h2', 'Hand vs machine embroidery bases'),
 ('p', 'Hand embroidery on an adda or frame keeps the fabric taut and can work on very fine bases with skilled artisans. Machine embroidery stitches faster and puts more tension on the fabric, so it usually needs firmer bases or stronger stabilisers. Multi-head machines also need consistent fabric widths and good hooping.'),
 ('h2', 'Weight and comfort'),
 ('p', 'Heavy embroidery can make a garment uncomfortable for long events. Estimate the finished weight early, especially for bridal and occasion pieces, and balance heavy areas with lighter ones. Linings in cotton or silk next to the skin improve comfort under metal threads and sequins.'),
 ('h2', 'Testing before bulk'),
 ('ul', ['Embroider a test panel on the actual fabric and lining.', 'Wash or dry-clean the panel as the customer will.', 'Check for puckering, colour bleeding and thread breakage.', 'Wear-test sleeves and seams where embroidery rubs against the body.']),
 ('h2', 'Global uses'),
 ('p', 'The same rules apply worldwide: embroidered georgette for Gulf eveningwear, lace on tulle for European bridal, and embroidered cotton for summer resort collections. Choosing the right base is what makes the work last.'),
],
'fabrics-hand-embroideries-of-india': [
 ('h2', 'Recognising each technique'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Chikankari:</b> fine white or tonal stitches, shadow work seen through sheer cotton.', '<b>Zardozi:</b> raised metallic work with coiled wire, sequins and beads.', '<b>Aari:</b> fine chain stitch made with a hooked needle, often on frames, quick and continuous.', '<b>Phulkari:</b> long darning stitches in floss silk that cover large areas.', '<b>Kantha:</b> running stitches that create texture and quilting.', '<b>Mirror work (shisha):</b> small mirrors held in place by stitched frames.']),
 ('h2', 'Price and time'),
 ('p', 'Hand embroidery is priced by the area covered, the stitch density and the technique. Dense zardozi can take many times longer than the same area of aari or kantha. Ask for time estimates per panel and plan production calendars around festival periods, when artisans may be busy or away.'),
 ('h2', 'Working fairly with artisans'),
 ('ul', ['Agree prices before work starts and pay on time.', 'Provide clear, final designs to avoid rework.', 'Respect regional techniques and avoid calling machine imitations by craft names.', 'Consider long-term partnerships rather than one-off orders.']),
 ('h2', 'International demand'),
 ('p', 'Indian hand embroidery is used by luxury brands around the world, often without credit. Brands that name the technique and region add authenticity. International buyers increasingly ask for documentation of working conditions, so keep records of who made what and on what terms.'),
 ('h2', 'Trend direction'),
 ('p', 'Forecut sees tonal hand work, focal placement (yokes, cuffs and borders) and lighter bases rising for SS27, with heavy all-over metal work concentrated in bridal.'),
],
'fabrics-woven-vs-knit': [
 ('h2', 'Common woven fabrics'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Poplin:</b> fine plain weave for shirts.', '<b>Twill:</b> diagonal weave for chinos and denim.', '<b>Satin:</b> long floats for sheen.', '<b>Oxford:</b> basket weave for casual shirts.', '<b>Canvas:</b> heavy plain weave for bags and outerwear.']),
 ('h2', 'Common knits'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Single jersey:</b> T-shirts; curls at the edges.', '<b>Rib:</b> stretchy, for cuffs, collars and fitted tops.', '<b>Interlock:</b> double knit, smooth both sides, more stable.', '<b>Piqué:</b> textured knit for polo shirts.', '<b>Fleece and French terry:</b> brushed or looped backs for sweatshirts.']),
 ('h2', 'Pattern and fit differences'),
 ('p', 'Knit patterns usually have negative or zero ease, because the fabric stretches. Woven patterns need positive ease and darts or seams for shape. A woven pattern sewn in a knit will be too big; a knit pattern sewn in a woven may not fit at all. Always grade and fit separately for each construction.'),
 ('h2', 'Stretch wovens'),
 ('p', 'Adding elastane or using mechanical stretch gives wovens some give while keeping a tailored look. They are popular in trousers, denim and commuter suiting. Check recovery: poor recovery leads to baggy knees and seats.'),
 ('h2', 'Climate and comfort'),
 ('p', 'In hot, humid climates, open wovens such as voile and linen and light knits such as cotton jersey breathe best. In cold climates, dense wovens block wind while knits trap warm air. Many garments combine both: a woven shell with a knit lining or rib trims.'),
],
'fabrics-indian-handloom-guide': [
 ('h2', 'Regional map'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Uttar Pradesh:</b> Banarasi silk and brocade from Varanasi.', '<b>Madhya Pradesh:</b> Chanderi and Maheshwari.', '<b>Rajasthan:</b> Kota doria.', '<b>West Bengal and Bangladesh:</b> Jamdani muslin.', '<b>Tamil Nadu:</b> Kanjeevaram silk.', '<b>Maharashtra:</b> Paithani.', '<b>Telangana:</b> Pochampally ikat.', '<b>Gujarat:</b> Patola double ikat; Kutch weaves.', '<b>Assam:</b> Muga and eri silks.']),
 ('h2', 'Geographical Indications'),
 ('p', 'Many Indian handloom textiles are registered as Geographical Indications (GI), which protects the name for products made in the region by traditional methods. Using a GI name for an imitation can mislead customers. When buying, ask for proof of origin, and use the protected name only for genuine products.'),
 ('h2', 'Handloom vs powerloom'),
 ('p', 'Handloom fabric is woven on a hand-operated loom, which gives small variations in tension and texture. Powerloom versions of the same designs are cheaper and more uniform. Both have a place, but they should be described honestly. India\'s Handloom Mark scheme helps identify genuine handloom products.'),
 ('h2', 'Selling handloom internationally'),
 ('ul', ['Lead with the story of place and maker, supported by documentation.', 'Offer simple silhouettes that let the weave lead.', 'Provide clear care guidance; many handlooms need gentle washing.', 'Show close-up texture images; customers abroad may not know the weave.']),
 ('h2', 'Handloom and climate'),
 ('p', 'Light cotton handlooms such as khadi, chanderi and jamdani are naturally suited to hot climates and are increasingly sought after in summer collections worldwide, from the Mediterranean to Australia.'),
],
'fabrics-knit-design-guide': [
 ('h2', 'Yarns and their character'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Merino wool:</b> soft, elastic, temperature-regulating; ideal for fine gauges.', '<b>Cashmere:</b> very soft and warm; premium price.', '<b>Cotton:</b> cool and breathable; less elastic, can stretch out.', '<b>Linen and linen blends:</b> dry, textured hand for summer knits.', '<b>Viscose and lyocell:</b> drape and sheen for fluid knits.', '<b>Recycled blends:</b> lower-impact options; check pilling and handle.']),
 ('h2', 'Fully fashioned and whole garment'),
 ('p', 'Cut-and-sew knits are made from knitted panels cut like woven fabric. Fully fashioned knits are shaped on the machine with fewer cut edges and a cleaner finish. Whole-garment (seamless) knitting produces a complete garment on the machine with almost no waste, but requires specialised machinery.'),
 ('h2', 'Common problems and fixes'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Pilling:</b> choose longer fibres and tighter structures.', '<b>Twisting side seams:</b> use balanced yarns and correct finishing.', '<b>Shrinkage:</b> test wash and specify relaxed dimensions.', '<b>Holes at linking:</b> check linking quality and yarn strength.']),
 ('h2', 'Knits by climate'),
 ('p', 'Fine-gauge cotton and linen knits extend the knit category into warm climates and summer. Mid-gauge wool covers transitional weather, and low-gauge chunky knits are for cold markets. For hot regions, pointelle and open stitches add airflow.'),
 ('h2', 'Briefing checklist'),
 ('p', 'Before sampling, agree on target weight, gauge, stitch, yarn, colour references, measurement chart and trims. A physical swatch or reference sample saves more time than any written description.'),
],
'fabrics-innovations': [
 ('h2', 'How to evaluate a new material'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Performance:</b> strength, pilling, shrinkage and colour fastness compared with what you use today.', '<b>Supply:</b> minimums, lead times and whether volumes can grow with you.', '<b>Cost:</b> price per metre and the effect on retail price.', '<b>Claims:</b> certifications and test reports that support any environmental claim.', '<b>End of life:</b> whether the material can be recycled or will biodegrade under real conditions.']),
 ('h2', 'Where each innovation fits'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Lyocell and modal:</b> shirts, dresses, underwear, blends with cotton and linen.', '<b>Recycled polyester and nylon:</b> outerwear, linings, swimwear and activewear.', '<b>Agri-waste fibres:</b> blends for shirting, home textiles and accessories.', '<b>Bio-based leather alternatives:</b> bags, small leather goods and footwear trims.', '<b>Low-impact colour:</b> digital printing for short runs, new dyeing for core basics.']),
 ('h2', 'Avoiding greenwashing'),
 ('p', 'Regulators in many markets now check environmental claims. Use precise wording ("made with 50% recycled polyester") instead of broad words such as "eco" or "sustainable". Keep the supporting documents in case a buyer or authority asks for them.'),
 ('h2', 'Global adoption'),
 ('p', 'European buyers often require recycled or certified materials. In North America, recycled synthetics are common in outdoor and activewear. In Asia, agri-waste fibres and recycled polyester are growing, supported by large manufacturing bases. Forecut\'s innovation radar marks each technology\'s stage and expected timing.'),
],
'fabrics-gsm-and-weight': [
 ('h2', 'How GSM is measured'),
 ('p', 'A circular cutter takes a sample of exactly 100 square centimetres, which is weighed on a precise scale; multiplying by 100 gives grams per square metre. Mills usually quote GSM with a tolerance, often around plus or minus 5%. Ask for the tolerance in writing and check bulk deliveries against it.'),
 ('h2', 'Other ways weight is described'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Ounces per square yard (oz):</b> common for denim; 1 oz/yd² is about 34 GSM.', '<b>Momme (mm):</b> used for silk; 1 momme is about 4.3 GSM.', '<b>Grams per linear metre:</b> depends on width, so convert before comparing.']),
 ('h2', 'Choosing weight by product'),
 ('ul', ['<b>T-shirts:</b> about 140–220 GSM; heavier for premium boxy tees.', '<b>Shirts:</b> about 90–140 GSM.', '<b>Summer dresses and kurtas:</b> about 70–130 GSM.', '<b>Sweatshirts:</b> about 260–380 GSM.', '<b>Denim jeans:</b> roughly 10–14 oz is common; lighter for summer, heavier for raw denim.', '<b>Coats:</b> often 400 GSM and above.']),
 ('h2', 'Weight by climate'),
 ('p', 'Hot and humid markets, such as South and Southeast Asia, the Gulf in summer and West Africa, favour lighter, open fabrics. Temperate markets use mid-weights across most of the year. Cold markets need heavier fabrics or layering. Offering the same style in two weights is an efficient way to serve several regions.'),
 ('h2', 'Weight and cost'),
 ('p', 'Fabric is often priced per metre or per kilogram. Heavier fabrics use more fibre and cost more to ship. When you change GSM to hit a price, check opacity, drape and durability, so the cheaper version still meets your quality standard.'),
],
'fabrics-weave-techniques': [
 ('h2', 'Plain weave in detail'),
 ('p', 'Each weft yarn passes over one warp yarn and under the next. Plain weave is the most common construction, used for poplin, voile, muslin, organza, chiffon and canvas. It is strong and stable, gives clear prints and has the same look on both sides. Variations include basket weave, where two or more yarns move together, as in oxford shirting and hopsack.'),
 ('h2', 'Twill weave in detail'),
 ('p', 'The weft passes over two or more warp yarns, stepping along each row to create a diagonal line. Twills, such as denim, gabardine, chino and drill, are durable and drape well, and hide soil better than plain weaves. Herringbone reverses the diagonal at intervals for a zigzag pattern.'),
 ('h2', 'Satin weave in detail'),
 ('p', 'Long floats, where a yarn passes over four or more yarns, create a smooth, shiny surface. Satin drapes beautifully and suits eveningwear and linings, but floats can snag. When the same weave uses cotton, it is often called sateen.'),
 ('h2', 'Where each weave is used'),
 ('ul', ['<b>Shirting:</b> poplin and oxford (plain and basket), end-on-end, small dobbies and seersucker for summer.', '<b>Trousers and outerwear:</b> twills such as gabardine, chino and drill; herringbone and tweed-like twills for winter.', '<b>Eveningwear and linings:</b> satin, taffeta (a crisp plain weave) and jacquard brocades.', '<b>Festive and occasion wear:</b> Banarasi and other brocades are jacquard-type weaves; velvet adds depth for winter weddings.', '<b>Summer bags and layers:</b> leno and open plain weaves for airflow.']),
 ('h2', 'Choosing a weave'),
 ('ul', ['Need durability and hidden wear? Choose twill.', 'Need a crisp, printable surface? Choose plain weave.', 'Need sheen and drape? Choose satin.', 'Need texture without print? Choose dobby, jacquard or seersucker.', 'Need airflow in hot climates? Choose open plain weaves, leno or seersucker.']),
 ('h2', 'Trend direction'),
 ('p', 'Forecut tracks seersucker, textured dobbies and lightweight jacquards rising for SS27, and upholstery-style jacquards and velvets for AW27 jackets and eveningwear.'),
],
}
